xt-gray-800 mb-1","children":"Detailed Vertigo Evaluation"}],"$L3b"]}]]}],"$L3c"]}]]}]]}]}]}]
17:["$","$L3d",null,{"faqItems":[{"q":"What is vertigo?","a":"Vertigo is a sensation that you or your surroundings are spinning, moving or tilting. It is a symptom rather than a single disease and can result from problems involving the inner ear, vestibular system, migraine or, less commonly, neurological conditions."},{"q":"What is the difference between vertigo and dizziness?","a":"Dizziness is a broad term that can describe lightheadedness, unsteadiness or a spinning sensation. Vertigo specifically refers to an illusion of movement, such as spinning, tilting or the surroundings moving."},{"q":"What is the most common cause of vertigo?","a":"BPPV is one of the most common causes of vertigo. It typically causes brief spinning episodes triggered by specific head movements, such as turning in bed, looking up or bending down."},{"q":"How long does vertigo last?","a":"The duration depends on the cause. BPPV commonly causes brief positional episodes, while some inner-ear disorders can cause prolonged symptoms lasting hours or days. Recurrent or persistent vertigo should be evaluated to identify the underlying cause."},{"q":"Can BPPV be treated without surgery?","a":"Yes. Appropriately diagnosed BPPV is commonly treated with canalith repositioning manoeuvres such as the Epley manoeuvre. The exact manoeuvre depends on the affected balance canal."},{"q":"Can vertigo cause hearing loss or tinnitus?","a":"Some inner-ear conditions can cause vertigo together with hearing loss, tinnitus or ear fullness. These associated symptoms are important during an ENT evaluation because they can help identify the likely cause."},{"q":"Can vertigo be completely cured?","a":"Some causes of vertigo respond very well to appropriate treatment, while other vestibular disorders may require longer-term management. The expected outcome depends on the underlying diagnosis, severity and response to treatment."},{"q":"How long does vertigo treatment take?","a":"Treatment time varies according to the cause. Some patients with BPPV may improve rapidly after appropriate repositioning, while vestibular rehabilitation or management of recurrent vestibular disorders may take several weeks or require ongoing follow-up."},{"q":"Should I see an ENT doctor for vertigo?","a":"An ENT specialist can be appropriate when vertigo is associated with ear symptoms, hearing changes, tinnitus, recurrent episodes or suspected vestibular disorders. Sudden vertigo with neurological warning signs requires urgent medical assessment."},{"q":"What happens if vertigo is left untreated?","a":"The consequences depend on the cause. Recurrent vertigo can contribute to falls, persistent imbalance, activity limitations and reduced quality of life. More importantly, unexplained or atypical symptoms may require assessment to identify the underlying condition."},{"q":"Can vertigo be caused by an ear problem?","a":"Yes. The inner ear contains structures involved in balance, so disorders such as BPPV, Ménière's disease, vestibular neuritis and labyrinthitis can cause vertigo."},{"q":"When is vertigo an emergency?","a":"Seek urgent medical attention if sudden vertigo occurs with weakness or numbness, facial drooping, speech difficulty, double vision, inability to stand or walk, severe headache or neck pain, confusion or other new neurological symptoms."},{"q":"Where can I consult an ENT specialist for vertigo in Delhi?","a":"Dr. Dhirendra Singh Kushwah provides ENT consultation for vertigo, dizziness, balance disorders and related ear conditions in South Delhi. Appointments are available at the listed clinic locations by appointment."},{"q":"How do I book a vertigo consultation in Delhi?","a":"You can contact Dr. Dhirendra Singh Kushwah's clinic by phone or WhatsApp at +91-9311449825, or email drdhirendrasinghent@gmail.com for appointment information."}]}]
